From d19ff16c11db38f3ee179d72751fb9b340174330 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Peter Zijlstra <peterz@infradead.org>
Date: Tue, 24 Feb 2026 17:35:37 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] hrtimer: Avoid pointless reprogramming in
 __hrtimer_start_range_ns()

Much like hrtimer_reprogram(), skip programming if the cpu_base is running
the hrtimer interrupt.

Scope note: 6.6.30 is the ONLY pinned base that still needs this patch.
Every other tree already ends in the same behavior by other means: 6.6.66,
6.6.89 (A15/A16/global) and 6.6.118 carry the newer upstream CSD-kick
rework of this region (hrtimer_base_is_online + smp_call_function_single_
async via hrtimer_base_csd) - a superset of this fix - and the Hipuu
6.6.142 fork pre-ships the in_hrtirq early-return itself. action.yml
therefore skips on either marker and applies this hunk with --fuzz=0.

The hunk is anchored to 6.6.30's exact region with real trailing context
(`if (!force_local)` / `return first;`) so the application is
self-verifying: with --fuzz=0, line drift or a wrong base fails loudly
instead of blindly inserting at the recorded line number. The first run
of this file carried only a single context line, and 6.6.118's build
wedged it into the middle of a stock comment at fuzz 1, failing to
compile hrtimer.c (run 33361879790). Blank context lines carry an
explicit space prefix: unprefixed empty lines are tolerated by GNU patch
but rejected by this repo's hunk-count validator.

Signed-off-by: Peter Zijlstra (Intel) <peterz@infradead.org>
Signed-off-by: Thomas Gleixner <tglx@kernel.org>
Reviewed-by: Juri Lelli <juri.lelli@redhat.com>
Reviewed-by: Thomas Gleixner <tglx@kernel.org>
Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20260224163429.069535561@kernel.org
---
 kernel/time/hrtimer.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/kernel/time/hrtimer.c b/kernel/time/hrtimer.c
--- a/kernel/time/hrtimer.c
+++ b/kernel/time/hrtimer.c
--- a/kernel/time/hrtimer.c
+++ b/kernel/time/hrtimer.c
@@ -1261,6 +1261,14 @@
 	}
 
 	first = enqueue_hrtimer(timer, new_base, mode);
+
+	/*
+	 * If the hrtimer interrupt is running, then it will reevaluate the
+	 * clock bases and reprogram the clock event device.
+	 */
+	if (new_base->cpu_base->in_hrtirq)
+		return false;
+
 	if (!force_local)
 		return first;
 
